417] [17592186124993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124993 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36488" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124993 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b5f682f-a0de-52f6-930d-b54c3a22eede" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124993 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-8)-9\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125361 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125361 #edb/ref 1061 17537 #